Node Breakdown
1. Schedule Trigger (One-Time Cron)
Fires once at 8:00 AM on October 6 using a specific cron expression
(0 0 8 6 10 *). This is a one-time scheduled execution — not a recurring job.
Useful for campaigns tied to a specific date where manual execution would be risky.
A manual trigger node runs in parallel, allowing the workflow to be tested or
re-executed without waiting for the scheduled time.

2. BDCompleta + Registrados (Parallel Sheet Reads)
Both Google Sheets are read simultaneously as soon as the trigger fires:

BDCompleta: Full master contact database
Registrados: List of contacts who already registered for the webinar

Both outputs feed into the deduplication node. Reading them in parallel reduces
total execution time compared to sequential reads.

3. "la magia" — JavaScript Deduplication Filter
The core logic node. A custom JavaScript function that:

Builds a Set of email addresses from the registered list
Iterates through every row in the full database
Skips any row where:

The email field is empty
The value is not a valid email format (regex validation)
The email already exists in the registered set
The email has already been seen in the current pass (deduplication within the DB)


Returns only clean, unregistered, unique email addresses

javascriptconst regSet = new Set(
  regs.map(i => clean(i.json['Correo'])).filter(Boolean)
);
// Skips registered, invalid, empty, and duplicate emails
Key design decisions:

Unicode normalization (\u00A0 non-breaking space removal) — handles
copy-paste artifacts common in spreadsheet data that would otherwise cause
false email mismatches
Case-insensitive comparison — toLowerCase() on all emails prevents
User@email.com and user@email.com from being treated as different addresses
Regex email validation — filters out malformed entries before they reach
the send step, preventing Outlook errors and bounce risk
In-loop deduplication — a secondary seen Set catches duplicates within the
master database itself, not just against the registered list


4. Loop Over Items (Batch Processing — 40 per batch)
Splits the filtered contact list into batches of 40 and processes them sequentially,
looping back after each batch completes.
Why batching? Microsoft Outlook's API has rate limits on bulk sending. Sending
all emails simultaneously would trigger throttling or rejections. Batches of 40
with a 90-second wait between each batch keeps the send rate well within limits.

5. Send Outlook Email
Sends a personalized HTML email to each contact via Microsoft Outlook OAuth. The
email:

Uses the contact's name dynamically from the sheet data
Contains full HTML formatting with a clear call-to-action
Has retryOnFail: true with a 5-second wait between retries — ensuring temporary
Outlook API errors don't silently drop emails
Uses onError: continueRegularOutput — a single failed send doesn't abort the
entire batch

Embedded tracking pixel:
html<img src="https://[n8n-instance]/webhook/[id]?email={{ $json.CorreoEnvio }}"
     width="1" height="1" style="display:none;" />
A 1×1 invisible image is embedded in every email. When the recipient opens the email,
their email client loads the image — firing the webhook with their email address as
a query parameter. This is standard email open tracking, implemented entirely within
n8n without any external analytics service.

6. Wait (90 seconds)
Pauses execution for 90 seconds between batches. Works in conjunction with the Loop
node to create a controlled send rate of ~40 emails per 90 seconds — well within
Outlook's rate limits.

7. Webhook + Apertura (Open Tracking Pipeline)
A parallel, always-on pipeline that runs independently of the send flow:

Webhook node listens at a fixed URL path for incoming pixel fire requests
Extracts the email query parameter from the request
Apertura node appends a row to a dedicated Google Sheet with:

Recipient email address
Date of open (yyyy-MM-dd)
Time of open (HH:mm:ss)



This creates a real-time open tracking log that the client can use to identify
engaged contacts for follow-up prioritization — functionally equivalent to what
dedicated email platforms like Mailchimp or ActiveCampaign provide, built entirely
in n8n.

Key Takeaways

Deduplication before send is non-negotiable for any email campaign workflow.
Sending to already-registered contacts damages trust and wastes quota. A JavaScript
Set-based filter is fast, reliable, and handles edge cases (unicode, case, invalid
formats) that simple string matching misses.
Batch + wait pattern is the correct approach for bulk email via API. Never send
all items simultaneously — always respect the target API's rate limits with batching
and deliberate pauses.
Self-hosted open tracking via pixel + webhook replicates a core email marketing
platform feature inside n8n at zero additional cost. Any workflow that sends emails
can be extended with this pattern to capture engagement data without a third-party
ESP.
Dual triggers (scheduled + manual) is good practice for any one-time campaign
workflow. The manual trigger allows safe testing and emergency re-execution without
modifying the schedule.
retryOnFail + continueRegularOutput on the send node is essential for
production email workflows. Individual send failures should never abort the entire
campaign — log them, continue, and review the failure log afterward.
